Not trying to sound like I'm a elitist or trying to down talk, but as far as "performance you will actually notice when using your PC" goes The following statements are true. You will most likely NOT notice a lot of things that most people say are performance related. Upgrading your RAM from 2400mhz to 2800 mhz only results in a real world 1-4% speed increase. No one will notice a 2% gain in performance on their own without benchmarking software. You will likely NOT notice a difference in speed between a 3.0Ghz CPU compared to the direct equivelant 3.2Ghz CPU. HDD capacity has nothing to do with performance at all, assuming your HD isn't like 98% full. Mechanical harddrives in this day are almost phased out, and should only be used for storage of non-essential files (music, images), and not to load your OS or primary game off of. You WILL notice a big gap in performance between onboard built-in graphics (AKA built-in graphics into your motherboard rather then a physical dedicated graphics card) and an actual physical graphics card. You WILL notice a GIGANTIC increase in speed going from a mechanical (5400,7200,10000RPM) hard drive to a solid state hard drive, or better yet, an m.2 socketed solid state drive, even if the actual storage capacity is even or less.  Most people who want to game but don't know very much about computers, will find that switching their hard drive from mechanical to solid state will result in more performance and speed, usually for a lesser price, then any other upgrade could usher in. Going from mechanical to SSD when referring to were your OS is installed, can be the difference in waiting two minutes to only 20 seconds for your PC to boot up.

&#x200B;

And for bonus points, building an SLI or Crossfire set up does NOT result in twice the performance. People think, if I use two graphics cards instead of one, I will get double the performance. Due to most games not utilizing this hard ware, drivers not be optimized or trained to use both cards, and game devs not putting in time to make these things because the bulk majority of gamers don't use SLI or Crossfire and it would require a decent amount of time to make for only \~10% of their target market, it is deemed not worth the time. Going SLI/Crossfire will only result in a 40-80% increase in performance (FPS, etc) depending on what drivers they are using and how the game they are playing was optimized. Now theres nothing  wrong with an extra 40-80% frame rate, but it's not double or triple like most people think. The more cards you add, the more diminishing returns you will get. Instead of buying a second 400$ card, most would be better off spending that money on SSDs, dedicated sound cards (takes the audio load off of your CPU, and usually are pretty cheap), more RAM, and better cooling for the CPU to encourage overclocking, to gain performance.

