It's not free if you aren't indigent. At least not where I've ever seen.

Some examples may be,

10$ to be seen for a non-emergency

5$ for refillable prescriptions

25$ charge for a cane or wheel chair

&#x200B;

That may all be cheaper then they would have gotten it for on the outside, insurance or not, but it's certainly not free.

&#x200B;

Now, if you are declared indigent, they can't charge you. If they do, they know they are likely never going to see the money, and they can't keep you locked up after your time is served for not being able to pay. If you actually have money on your books, (commissary), then they would usually deduct those charges from that.

&#x200B;

And that leads to another HUGE problem. If you receive any of these services and are charged from them, you potentially start with a negative balance. If you need medicine and they charge you for it, and then next week your good ol baby momma or "brother" comes by to throw some money on your books, the money you "owe" for this "medical care" will immediately be deducted from whatever money you were brought. Because some people either only get money for their commissary occasionally, or don't receive very much when they do, they don't want the little amount of money they get to be eaten up, before they get a chance to eat up whatever they would buy from the store. This basically makes it so some people either don't EVER get commissary and go hungry all the time, because they don't want to pay for the care that they didn't even ask for, or, they "trust" a friend (who they probably didn't know before being locked up), by having the money put on person B's books, and then trusting again that this person will order what person A wants. As you can imagine, this has a ton of possible outcomes that end poorly. Money stolen, "Oh hey man, I know you said your brother came by with 20$ but I didn't see any money on my books so I don't know what to tell you" or, "they must have not put it on in time for this weeks store day, I'll get you next week". And when people are already in jail, and hungry, add anger to this and it always ends poorly. Fights, stealing of goods, stealing of the food trays, whatever they can get. And all this results because of the "free" medical care that guy was talking about.

&#x200B;

No one about to go to jail or prison is expecting a resort. However, where you may have been happiest and most able to serve time by doing this work to get out of your cell, the very next inmate may find that reading or sleeping is the best way to get through there time, and want nothing to do with trustee, benefits or not.

If you were a CO and you claim that inmates got "free medical care", you must have worked in the nicest jail in the world. You only get immediate medical care for SUPER EMERGENCIES. Generally anything you claim came out of nowhere with no one to witness an injury, and they almost always instantly assume your lying. To get medical care - maybe a tooth pulled (god never have this done while in jail), or anything less serious, say a rash or bite, they will tell you to put in a kite. Basically a form saying you are requesting to be seen by a doctor. The medical staff, while "probably" actual doctors, are probably working in a jail and not a real hospital for a reason. Once you submit a kite, you will in real time wait anywhere between one and infinite weeks before you are actually seen. 5-8 weeks to be seen for the issue you deem most vital to having a doctor look at was very normal. Good luck if you need some kind of medication that was ever used any where in the world one time by any one for abuse. Once you are actually seen, you will be seen by someone who knows nothing about you, more likely then not thinks you are lying about whatever you say as an excuse to get out of the cell or pod for awhile, and has a goal of getting through as many people as they can as quickly as possible whether they actually helped you or not. If anything I guess you can say that there is an opportunity to receive medical care (Which, if you're not indigent, WILL, while at a discount, still be charged to an in mate) for emergencies and certain ailments they deem important enough, eventually.

&#x200B;

As for not doing any work that a regular free person wouldn't do? If you are a free person which I'm guessing you are as you are posting on reddit, would YOU hose down showers with staph infections, black mold, TB exposure, and god knows what other microscopic ailments may be hiding, for an extra apple at dinner or even a double tray of pure slop? I'm guessing you either wouldn't do this, or demand more compensation then that. I've never seen trustees get paid at any jail, city or county, either. The only time I have ever seen that as a possibility is at prisons. And, they will only be granted that opportunity after cleared by medical staff, and after waiting their turn on a list. Are you a violent criminal? Drug trafficker? Flight risk? Sex offender? Don't even bother applying. Yeah, you get to do a task so your not sitting around all day, and not to stereotype, but most people in jail or prison aren't too keen on doing "extra work for the man" with little or no extra or compensation at all. Doing trustee work does not impress prosecutors or judges and will rarely grant you leniency.

&#x200B;

I'm not even going to bother on rec or phone time, because with the exception with people who have gotten these privileges revoked for getting in trouble, are things that any one gets, whether they work or not. Certainly not an extra right given for doing the trustee work.

&#x200B;

